WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.940
A fruit fly and a frog look nothing alike.

2
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.700
Do they build a nervous system the same way?

3
00:00:09.683 --> 00:00:11.243
The fly has the same pair.

4
00:00:12.103 --> 00:00:16.283
Its gene decapentaplegic encodes a relative of BMP2 and 4.

5
00:00:17.267 --> 00:00:21.087
And short gastrulation, or Sog, encodes a relative of Chordin.

6
00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:25.750
So Dpp plays the part of the frog's BMP4.

7
00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:28.210
Sog plays the part of its Chordin.

8
00:00:29.233 --> 00:00:34.033
In 1995, Holley and colleagues swapped the two antagonists between species.

9
00:00:35.217 --> 00:00:37.017
Frog Chordin works in the fly.

10
00:00:37.017 --> 00:00:39.457
And fly Sog works in the frog.

11
00:00:40.417 --> 00:00:43.697
Tolloid was first named as a fly gene, and it cuts Sog.

12
00:00:44.617 --> 00:00:49.197
Now the map. In the fly, Dpp is made on the dorsal side.

13
00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:54.227
Sog is made in broad lateral stripes of ectoderm on the ventral half.

14
00:00:55.217 --> 00:00:58.677
Use the rule. Where should the fly's nerve cord form?

15
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:09.080
Ventrally, from that ectoderm, where Sog keeps Dpp signaling low.

16
00:01:12.583 --> 00:01:14.023
The picture is simplified.

17
00:01:14.983 --> 00:01:17.983
The ventral-most fly cells are mesoderm, not nerve cord.

18
00:01:18.983 --> 00:01:23.223
In a vertebrate, BMP4 signaling is high on the ventral side.

19
00:01:24.183 --> 00:01:28.563
Chordin comes from the dorsal organizer, and the neural tube forms dorsally.

20
00:01:29.567 --> 00:01:35.347
In both animals, nervous system forms where the antagonist keeps the BMP signal low.

21
00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:38.390
What happens if you turn the fly over?

22
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.980
Its BMP and its antagonist lie where frog's do.

23
00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:49.960
The two maps line up.

24
00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:59.770
In 1996, De Robertis and Sasai argued the common ancestor already patterned its body

25
00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:00.770
this way.

26
00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.490
One lineage then inverted the plan.

27
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:09.460
On this view, the fly's map and the frog's map are one ancestral map, read from

28
00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:10.460
opposite sides.

29
00:02:11.283 --> 00:02:16.783
Long before, in the 1820s, Étienne Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire had proposed this

30
00:02:16.783 --> 00:02:18.403
inversion from anatomy alone.

31
00:02:19.433 --> 00:02:24.773
The takeaway: fly and frog run the same contest, BMP against its catcher.

32
00:02:25.153 --> 00:02:26.633
Only the body
